description:
Build `Heap<T, Less>`, a binary heap in a `std::vector` whose `top()` is the element that is **smallest** according to `Less` (so `Heap<int>` is a min-heap and `Heap<int, std::greater<int>>` a max-heap), then use it:

```cpp
template <class T, class Less = std::less<T>>
class Heap
{
public:
    Heap(Less less = Less{});
    explicit Heap(std::vector<T> items, Less less = Less{});   // heapify in O(n)
    void push(T value);
    T pop();                       // removes and returns the top; std::out_of_range if empty
    const T& top() const;          // std::out_of_range if empty
    std::size_t size() const;
    bool empty() const;
    bool valid() const;            // true if every parent is not greater than its children (for the tests)
};

std::vector<int> kLargest(const std::vector<int>& values, std::size_t k);      // the k largest, largest first, O(n log k)
std::vector<int> mergeSorted(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& lists);       // k-way merge with a heap, O(n log k)
```

- Store the tree in the vector: children of `i` at `2i + 1` and `2i + 2`.
- The vector constructor must **heapify** bottom-up (sift down from the last parent to the root), not push one by one.
- `kLargest` keeps a min-heap of at most `k` elements.
- `mergeSorted` puts (value, list index, position) triples in a min-heap.
- No `std::priority_queue` or `std::make_heap`/`push_heap`/`pop_heap` here.

